{
  "modality": "image",
  "analogy": {
    "title": "An editor with track changes",
    "text": "Picture a document passed down a line of 34 editors. Rewriting the whole text from scratch each time would garble it. Instead, each editor gets the current draft and only marks small changes in track-changes mode, and those changes are merged back into the draft. If an editor has nothing useful to add, they leave the draft as it is. ResNet works the same way: every block keeps its input through a shortcut and only learns a small correction F(x) that gets added on top. Because no editor can ruin the draft, the chain can be made very long."
  },
